\section*{A universal joint critical SK equilibrium and quench autocorrelation law}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: Critical stationary SK autocorrelation is claimed to have a common random functional limit for Gaussian and sign couplings after time scaling by $n^{2/3}$ and amplitude by $n^{1/3}$; The critical SK quench from independent fair spins is claimed to share a functional scaling law for Gaussian and sign couplings and to relax to the stationary limit at long waiting times. Together they give a claimed affirmative answer in the matching scope.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For independent standard Gaussian or fair Rademacher $J_{ij}$, $i<j$, with $J_{ji}=J_{ij},J_{ii}=0$, use the zero-field SK measure
\[
 \pi_{n,\beta}^J(x)=Z^{-1}\exp\left(\frac\beta{\sqrt n}\sum_{i<j}J_{ij}x_ix_j\right),\qquad x\in\{-1,1\}^n.
\]
Continuous heat-bath dynamics refreshes every site at rate one; a discrete step refreshes one uniformly selected site, including attempts that retain its spin. At $\beta=1$, write $P_t^J$ for the rate-one-per-site semigroup, and let
\[
 C_{n,J}(t)=n^{-1}\sum_i\langle x_i,P_t^Jx_i\rangle_\pi,\qquad
 A_{n,J}(s)=n^{1/3}C_{n,J}(sn^{2/3}).
\]
There exists a common probability law $Q_{\mathrm{crit}}$ on $C_{\mathrm{loc}}((0,\infty))$ such that $\operatorname{Law}(A_{n,J})\Longrightarrow Q_{\mathrm{crit}}$ for both interaction distributions. This law is nondegenerate and supported on nonzero, nonnegative, nonincreasing continuous functions tending to zero at infinity. The topology is uniform convergence on compact positive-time intervals.

\paragraph{Result 2.} For independent standard Gaussian or fair Rademacher $J_{ij}$, $i<j$, with $J_{ji}=J_{ij},J_{ii}=0$, use the zero-field SK measure
\[
 \pi_{n,\beta}^J(x)=Z^{-1}\exp\left(\frac\beta{\sqrt n}\sum_{i<j}J_{ij}x_ix_j\right),\qquad x\in\{-1,1\}^n.
\]
Continuous heat-bath dynamics refreshes every site at rate one; a discrete step refreshes one uniformly selected site, including attempts that retain its spin. At $\beta=1$, start the quench from uniform independent spins $\nu_n$, independent of $J$. With $T_n=n^{2/3}$ and heat-bath semigroup $P_t^J$, define
\[
 A_{n,J}(t)=n^{-2/3}\sum_i\langle x_i,P_{tT_n}^Jx_i\rangle_\pi,\quad
 B_{n,J}(s,t)=n^{-2/3}\sum_i\mathbb E_{\nu_n}^J[x_i(sT_n)x_i((s+t)T_n)].
\]
To specify the common limit, let $(g_a)_{a\ge1}$ be the increasing GOE edge process, normalized by $n^{2/3}(2-\lambda_a)\Longrightarrow g_a$. For $b+g_1>0$, set $\ell_a=(g_a+b)^{-1}$ and
\[
 D_g(b)=\lim_{L\to\infty}\left[\sqrt L-\sum_a\left((g_a+b)^{-1}-(g_a+L)^{-1}\right)\right].
\]
The defining limit for $D_g(b)$ is in probability over the edge process; an almost-sure version is fixed along a deterministic sequence of $L$. For independent $Z_a\sim N(0,\ell_a)$, let $\Pi_g$ be their law conditioned, by continuous densities, on $\sum_a(Z_a^2-\ell_a)=D_g(b)$; it is independent of the admissible $b$. Let $H_g$ generate the closed cylinder-gradient form $\int\sum_a|\partial_aF|^2\,d\Pi_g$, and set $S_t^g=e^{-tc_*H_g}$ with the common positive microscopic coefficient $c_*$. There are measurable densities $h_s^g\in L^2(\Pi_g)$ with
\[
 h_s^g\ge0,\quad\Pi_g[h_s^g]=1,\quad h_{s+t}^g=S_t^gh_s^g,\quad
 h_s^g\Pi_g\Longrightarrow\delta_0\ (s\downarrow0),\quad
 \|h_s^g-1\|_{L^1(\Pi_g)}\to0\ (s\to\infty).
\]
Here the initial convergence uses the product topology. The locally uniformly convergent series
\[
 A_g(t)=\sum_a\int x_aS_t^gx_a\,d\Pi_g,\qquad
 B_g(s,t)=\sum_a\int h_s^gx_aS_t^gx_a\,d\Pi_g
\]
define continuous limits, and for either interaction law
\[
 (A_{n,J},B_{n,J})\Longrightarrow(A_g,B_g)
 \quad\text{in }C_{\mathrm{loc}}((0,\infty))\times C_{\mathrm{loc}}((0,\infty)^2).
\]
For every $0<a<b<\infty$, $\sup_{a\le t\le b}|B_g(s,t)-A_g(t)|\to0$ in probability as $s\to\infty$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A conditioned spectral-edge measure and quantitative observation estimates control variance at the cap scale. Microscopic relaxation identifies the effective coefficient, resolvent-trace convergence yields the Gaussian process limit, and coupling comparison transports it to sign disorder.

Bottom-edge Gaussian estimates control warming on the critical clock. Positive-time limits select an entrance law from the uniform initial state, while smooth truncation of dynamical histories permits disorder replacement; comparison with the stationary edge input identifies the eventual relaxation.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The quench-universality manuscript claims the joint Gaussian/sign-coupling limit for the equilibrium process $A$ and quench process $B$, together with the entrance-law and long-waiting-time assertions in the question. The stationary functional-universality companion covers only $A$. The full classification uses the quench theorem, which supplies the clauses absent from the stationary companion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 227, the selected statements include the high-temperature gap for $\beta<1$, cutoff ratios only for $\beta<1/2$, critical lower mixing scales with a subexponential upper bound, and low-temperature barriers. They do not provide the full critical exponent, the full cutoff location, or the newer autocorrelation limits. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

